  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, preferring the more right-skewed lottery (A), which has a smaller chance of a larger loss. The individual considers the same attributes—probability and size of loss—but reaches a conclusion opposite to what the reason would dictate: they prefer B over A. Moreover, their justification emphasizes avoiding the large loss in A and being willing to accept a higher probability of loss for a smaller damage, which aligns more with loss aversion or minimizing maximum loss rather than favoring right-skewness. The individual does not consider the concept of skewness or distributional imbalance in their reasoning. There is no evidence they evaluated which lottery concentrates more probability on unfavorable outcomes relative to favorable ones in a skewed way. Instead, they treat the 0.1% vs 0.2% difference as small and focus on the magnitude of the loss. Therefore, the reasoning pattern does not match the decision reason. The information considered overlaps partially (probabilities and outcomes), but the rule applied (avoid large losses even at slightly higher probability) contradicts the skewness reason, which would favor the very option (A) the individual seems to reject. Thus, it is certain that the individual did not use the skewness-based reason.",
